1
00:00:01,800 --> 00:00:05,410
Not everyone who travels to Kochi is on holiday.

2
00:00:05,460 --> 00:00:09,030
Some come because someone they love has been admitted to hospital.

3
00:00:09,080 --> 00:00:12,400
And for the rest of the family, one question arrives quickly:

4
00:00:12,450 --> 00:00:13,970
where do we stay?

5
00:00:14,020 --> 00:00:16,217
That's the moment I wanted to understand.

6
00:00:17,317 --> 00:00:21,417
I'm building The Veedu, an accommodation discovery platform for Kerala.

7
00:00:21,467 --> 00:00:28,457
My background is in UX design and running rental accommodation, and I already had a working prototype.

8
00:00:28,507 --> 00:00:30,517
But a prototype isn't evidence.

9
00:00:30,567 --> 00:00:35,152
So before building any further, I set out to find whether it deserved to exist.

10
00:00:36,252 --> 00:00:38,412
My starting hypothesis was simple.

11
00:00:38,462 --> 00:00:44,062
Medical travellers and their families need things general booking platforms don't surface well:

12
00:00:44,112 --> 00:00:48,552
being close to the hospital, staying for weeks, and knowing the real cost.

13
00:00:48,602 --> 00:00:53,127
I wrote down what would prove that wrong before I looked for anything that proved it right.

14
00:00:54,227 --> 00:00:57,267
The official data gave me context — and a warning.

15
00:00:57,317 --> 00:01:07,877
Kerala recorded around two and a half crore domestic tourist visits in 2025, and in 2023, Ernakulam, Kochi's district, led the state.

16
00:01:07,927 --> 00:01:14,207
About six point four lakh foreigners came to India for medical treatment in 2024.

17
00:01:14,257 --> 00:01:16,827
But tourist visits aren't medical travellers.

18
00:01:16,877 --> 00:01:21,037
And the Ministry of Tourism doesn't keep state-wise medical tourism data.

19
00:01:21,087 --> 00:01:23,118
The number I needed most didn't exist.

20
00:01:24,218 --> 00:01:25,588
So I went to the ground:

21
00:01:25,638 --> 00:01:30,298
Amrita Hospital and Aster Medcity, in the Edappally–Cheranalloor corridor.

22
00:01:30,348 --> 00:01:36,498
At Amrita, one family member can stay in the patient's room, and other relatives are pointed to a guest house.

23
00:01:36,548 --> 00:01:44,068
I mapped twelve providers nearby, from budget rooms to a Marriott, and some already market to patients and bystanders.

24
00:01:44,118 --> 00:01:45,998
So this wasn't an empty market.

25
00:01:46,048 --> 00:01:49,312
The question changed from whether there's supply, to what's missing.

26
00:01:50,412 --> 00:01:52,462
The reviews were where it got interesting.

27
00:01:52,512 --> 00:01:59,772
I could only read twenty-five excerpts, well short of the hundred I wanted, so I treat them as a signal, not a statistic.

28
00:01:59,822 --> 00:02:01,882
Guests praised staff and cleanliness.

29
00:02:01,932 --> 00:02:06,992
They complained about extra charges at checkout, and maintenance on longer stays.

30
00:02:07,042 --> 00:02:10,502
And when reviewers mentioned the hospital, they wrote about distance.

31
00:02:10,552 --> 00:02:16,701
One guest house named after Amrita was a twelve-minute walk to one guest — and a little away to another.

32
00:02:17,801 --> 00:02:20,601
That became the insight everything else builds on.

33
00:02:20,651 --> 00:02:26,421
Distance is why families choose a stay, and it's the fact listings state least consistently.

34
00:02:26,471 --> 00:02:29,571
To size the market, I started with something I could count:

35
00:02:29,621 --> 00:02:32,481
over two thousand beds across the two hospitals.

36
00:02:32,531 --> 00:02:35,421
Every step after that is an explicit assumption.

37
00:02:35,471 --> 00:02:44,196
The base case projects around sixteen crore rupees a year in accommodation spend, with about eight crore a platform could realistically serve.

38
00:02:45,296 --> 00:02:46,736
Then the harder question:

39
00:02:46,786 --> 00:02:48,236
could a small pilot work?

40
00:02:48,286 --> 00:03:00,226
With ten partner properties and a twelve percent commission, the base case projects about seven lakh in platform revenue, and falls short of break-even, which needs around fifty-five bookings a month.

41
00:03:00,276 --> 00:03:03,026
Short, cheap stays lose money on every booking.

42
00:03:03,076 --> 00:03:08,016
The model only works for longer stays — the same families the research pointed to.

43
00:03:08,066 --> 00:03:12,438
So the pilot should be small, founder-led, and built to measure, not to scale.

44
00:03:13,538 --> 00:03:15,468
From there, the strategy narrowed.

45
00:03:15,518 --> 00:03:22,578
The primary customer is an out-of-town family caring for a patient admitted at Amrita or Aster Medcity.

46
00:03:22,628 --> 00:03:24,618
Their problem isn't finding a room.

47
00:03:24,668 --> 00:03:26,988
It's trusting the facts behind it.

48
00:03:27,038 --> 00:03:29,328
So the position I want to earn is simple:

49
00:03:29,378 --> 00:03:32,723
know how far, how much, and for how long, before you call.

50
00:03:33,823 --> 00:03:37,943
I scored nine features on reach, impact, confidence and effort.

51
00:03:37,993 --> 00:03:39,623
The MVP keeps five:

52
00:03:39,673 --> 00:03:49,963
hospital-based discovery, verified distance to the entrance, all-in pricing for the whole family, dated verification, and tracked WhatsApp enquiries.

53
00:03:50,013 --> 00:03:52,053
Online booking and payment can wait.

54
00:03:52,103 --> 00:03:58,264
And because my own prototype still shows straight-line distance, verification is the first thing to build.

55
00:03:59,364 --> 00:04:04,064
None of this is validated yet, so the next stage is a set of small experiments.

56
00:04:04,114 --> 00:04:06,534
Interview caregivers near both hospitals.

57
00:04:06,584 --> 00:04:10,364
Talk to hospital coordinators about how families are referred.

58
00:04:10,414 --> 00:04:14,324
Verify ten properties in person, and measure what it costs.

59
00:04:14,374 --> 00:04:23,143
Then run a few weeks of WhatsApp-led matching, tracking how many enquiries become confirmed stays, and how many families go direct the next time.

60
00:04:24,243 --> 00:04:25,853
I haven't proven a business.

61
00:04:25,903 --> 00:04:33,883
What I've done is narrow an idea I care about into a specific customer, a specific problem, and a test small enough to run.

62
00:04:33,933 --> 00:04:35,433
For me, that's the job.

63
00:04:35,483 --> 00:04:42,014
Not to be right at the start — but to find out, honestly and as cheaply as possible, what deserves to be built.
